      "text": "To determine the number of different 5-person starting lineups possible, we need to consider the combination of the 12 players given the two mandatory All-Stars, Ace and Zeppo.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of initial choices we have for players without considering the restrictions. Since Ace and Zeppo are already included, we start with the remaining 10 players.\n\nNext, we need to select the remaining 3 players from these 10 players to form a 5-person lineup, with the number of ways to choose these players calculated using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!} \\).\n\nThe total number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n\\]\n\nFactorial values cancel as \\(10! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!\\) and the two 7!s get canceled out:\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{720}{6} = 120\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 120 different ways to select the remaining 3 players from the 10 available players. Therefore, the total number of different possible 5-person starting lineups is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]",
