      "text": "We can find the number of ways of choosing 3 players out of 10.  (Filling out the line without Aces and Zeppos is equivalent to filling out a lineup with only 10 players available.)  There will be \\frac{10!}{3!7!} = \\boxed{120} positions in the starting lineup.\n\nSolve the following problem: What is the smallest number that is a multiple of both 15 and 9?\n\nSolution:\nWe can compare factors of 15 and 9 to see which has the smallest common multiple.\nThe factors of 15 are 1, 3, 5, and 15.\nThe factors of 9 are 1, 3, and 9.\nThe number 3 is the smallest factor that both 15 and 9 have in common.\nThis means that the smallest number that is a multiple of both 15 and 9 is $3 \\cdot 3 = \\boxed{9}$.\n\n(Note: The new problem asks for the smallest number that is a multiple of both 15 and 9, and not to find the greatest common divisor of 15 and 9, as those are two different questions!)",
